From: limina <gustavo@noronha.eti.br>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/virtio: align host-visible window allocations to 16 KiB

On hypervisors whose host page size is larger than the guest's - a 4 KiB
arm64 guest on a 16 KiB Apple Silicon host (Hypervisor.framework via
libkrun, or KVM on Asahi's 16 KiB kernels) - the host can only map blob
resources into the host-visible window at host-page granularity:
hv_vm_map / KVM memslots reject any guest address that is not a multiple
of the host page.

drm_mm_insert_node() packs vram nodes back to back at guest PAGE_SIZE
granularity, so the first blob whose size is not a multiple of the host
page shifts every subsequently packed node to a host-page-misaligned
window offset - and mapping those blobs fails unpredictably from then on
(venus: vkMapMemory -> VK_ERROR_MEMORY_MAP_FAILED mid-session). See
limina's l1_blob_map test (blob2, the offset half) and the memory
limina-blob-map-16k-alignment; the size half is handled host-side by
libkrun patch 0043.

Give every node a 16 KiB start alignment. With aligned starts the
packing order stops mattering: the next aligned start is always >= the
previous node's host-rounded map end, so no allocation history can
produce a misaligned offset or an overlapping host mapping, and hole
reuse stays aligned by induction. The node SIZE stays exact - the size
half is rounded host-side (libkrun 0043), and virtgpu_vram_mmap
requires the userspace mapping length to equal the node size. On 4 KiB-host hypervisors the alignment is pure
bounded slack (at most 12 KiB per allocation; mappable allocations are
few and large in practice - Vulkan drivers suballocate).

16 KiB covers every Apple Silicon host, the only deployment where the
mismatch exists today. An upstream submission would likely use 64 KiB
(the largest arm64 page size) or negotiate the granule; this carry patch
targets our hosts. The same delta ships out of tree as the
limina-virtio-gpu DKMS module for stock-kernel guests.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/virtio/virtgpu_vram.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/virtio/virtgpu_vram.c
index 25df81c..8c16020 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/virtio/virtgpu_vram.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/virtio/virtgpu_vram.c
@@ -2,6 +2,18 @@
 #include "virtgpu_drv.h"
 
 #include <linux/dma-mapping.h>
+#include <linux/sizes.h>
+
+/*
+ * Host mappings into the host-visible window happen at HOST page granularity;
+ * 16 KiB covers Apple Silicon hosts (the largest-page hosts we target) and is
+ * harmless slack elsewhere. Aligning every node's START is sufficient: the
+ * next aligned start is always >= the previous node's host-rounded map end,
+ * so no allocation history can produce a misaligned or overlapping host
+ * mapping. (The node size stays exact - virtgpu_vram_mmap requires the
+ * userspace mapping length to equal it.)
+ */
+#define VIRTIO_GPU_VRAM_ALIGN SZ_16K
 
 static void virtio_gpu_vram_free(struct drm_gem_object *obj)
 {
@@ -150,8 +162,9 @@ static int virtio_gpu_vram_map(struct virtio_gpu_object *bo)
 		return -EINVAL;
 
 	spin_lock(&vgdev->host_visible_lock);
-	ret = drm_mm_insert_node(&vgdev->host_visible_mm, &vram->vram_node,
-				 bo->base.base.size);
+	ret = drm_mm_insert_node_generic(&vgdev->host_visible_mm,
+					 &vram->vram_node, bo->base.base.size,
+					 VIRTIO_GPU_VRAM_ALIGN, 0, 0);
 	spin_unlock(&vgdev->host_visible_lock);
 
 	if (ret)
